370 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 948 0 R
/ActualText (This research explores the techno-economic feasibility of hybrid power plants to provide electricity in rural and underserved areas of India, Myanmar, Ghana, and Cameroon. It identifies optimal hybrid systems, including PV/WT/DG/BAT for India, Ghana, and Cameroon, and WT/DG/BAT for Myanmar. India's Levelized Cost of Energy \(LCOE\) is $0.127/kWh for stand-alone systems and $0.026/kWh for grid-tied systems, with significant cost savings reflected in the Net Present Cost \(NPC\). The study highlights the crucial role of sound energy policies in improving energy access, reducing costs, and promoting renewables in South Asia and Sub-Saharan Africa.[1])
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
371 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 949 0 R
/ActualText (Khiriya Bharka, a small village in Isagarh tehsil, Ashoknagar region, Madhya Pradesh, has 320 residents and 80 houses. The electrical load includes domestic appliances like lights, fans, mobile chargers, and televisions. Public buildings, such as schools, community halls, health clinics, and streetlights, contribute to the load. The residential and community demand for the 80 houses is estimated to be 286.95 kWh/day, with a peak load of 28.67 kW, designed to handle summer's higher peak demand. Commercial loads from stores and flour mills, consisting of lights, fans, and motors, require 262.15 kWh/day, with a peak load of 87.53 kW.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
